Tests that incorporate generators and inherit from SeededTest can pass even if they should fail. This issue occurs for me both locally and on Travis. For example, this test should clearly never pass:
from .helpers import SeededTest

class TestClassThatShouldFail(SeededTest):
    def test_that_should_fail(self):
        assert 0
        for i in [1/0]:
            assert 0
            yield self.failing_check
        assert 0

    def failing_check(self):
        assert 0
However, it does pass on both Python 2 and Python 3. This seems to affect the tests in, for example, test_distributions.py, where I can't get a test that includes a yield to fail.
Maybe it has something to do with method generators not being supported for subclasses of unittest.TestCase? Either not subclassing from SeededTest or removing the yield both cause the test to fail as expected.

Hi,
It's been a while since I started working with Dawid-Skene model for multiple annotator vote aggregation. In nutshell, bunch of annotators with different skills cast their votes of category on work items. Work items are of different difficulty as well. The goal is to infer the latent category (distribution over categories) for each work item.
I found this Stan model to be working pretty well on large datasets. That being said, I have troubles trying to port it to PyMC3 due to the following reasons:

How to deal with 1- and 2- dimensional arrays of random variables which are themselves of some shape?
How to implement the "transformed parameters" block part where the category variable is summed out?
How to implement & use likelihood which is a log sum of exponentials?

For 1st, the idea is to use T.stack but I quickly got lost in shapes, dimensions and cryptic error messages. For 2nd, I think I should use theano tensor but I have very little experience here. For 3rd, I would use Potential but I am not sure.
For me, I found the beta and theta variables to be problematic.
Here is the Stan model that I am using (thanks to Bob Carpenter for advice)
`data {
int<lower=2> K;              // number of categories
int<lower=1> I;              // number of items
int<lower=1> J;              // number of coders
int<lower=1> N;              // number of observations
int<lower=1,upper=J> jj[N];  // coder for observation n
int<lower=1,upper=I> ii[N];  // item for observation n
int<lower=1,upper=K> y[N];   // label for observation n
vector<lower=0>[K] alpha;    // prior for prevalence (positive)
vector<lower=0>[K] beta[K];  // prior for coder responses (positive)
}
parameters {
simplex[K] pi;               // prevalence of categories
simplex[K] theta[J,K];       // response of anotator j to category k
}
transformed parameters {
real cat_log[I,K];           // vector of log probs (up to const) for item i
for (i in 1:I)
for (k in 1:K)
cat_log[i,k] <- log(pi[k]);  // efficiency: compute log(pi[k]) once
for (n in 1:N)
for (k in 1:K)
cat_log[ii[n],k] <- cat_log[ii[n],k] + log(theta[jj[n],k,y[n]]);
}
model {
pi ~ dirichlet(alpha);
for (j in 1:J)
for (k in 1:K)
theta[j,k] ~ dirichlet(beta[k]);
for (i in 1:I)
increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(cat_log[i]));
}`
